          <div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 mb-3">1. What is Canine Distemper Virus (CDV)?</h2>
            <p>
              <strong>Canine distemper</strong> is an extremely contagious, incurable viral illness caused by an enveloped, single-stranded RNA paramyxovirus closely related to the human measles virus. In Pakistan's urban centers—including Lahore, Rawalpindi, Islamabad, and Karachi—distemper remains one of the top two fatal viral diseases in young dogs, standing alongside <a href="/blog/parvovirus-treatment-cost-survival-pakistan" class="text-emerald-700 font-bold underline">Canine Parvovirus</a>.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-3">
              The virus is transmitted primarily through airborne aerosol droplets when an infected dog coughs, sneezes, or barks. It can also spread through shared water bowls, bedding, kennels, and human footwear. Because stray dog populations in Pakistan frequently harbor the pathogen, unvaccinated household puppies, Persian breeds, German Shepherds, and Rottweilers are at severe risk.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 mb-3">2. Clinical Symptoms: The Two Stages of Distemper</h2>
            <p>
              Unlike simple kennel cough or bacterial bronchitis, distemper is a progressive biphasic infection that systematically attacks multiple organ systems:
            </p>
            
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mt-4">
              <div class="bg-white p-5 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
                <div class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-wider text-amber-600 mb-2">Stage 1: Respiratory &amp; Gastrointestinal (Days 3–14)</div>
                <ul class="space-y-2 text-xs text-slate-700 list-disc list-inside">
                  <li><strong>Spiking High Fever (103.5°F – 105.5°F):</strong> Often appears in two peaks (biphasic fever).</li>
                  <li><strong>Copious Eye &amp; Nose Discharge:</strong> Starts watery, then turns into thick, crusty yellow-green mucopurulent discharge.</li>
                  <li><strong>Persistent Moist Coughing &amp; Dyspnea:</strong> Secondary bacterial pneumonia is extremely common.</li>
                  <li><strong>Vomiting &amp; Diarrhea:</strong> Puppies refuse food and become rapidly dehydrated.</li>
                  <li><strong>Hyperkeratosis (&quot;Hard Pad Disease&quot;):</strong> Thickening, hardening, and cracking of the paw pads and nasal leather.</li>
                </ul>
              </div>

              <div class="bg-white p-5 rounded-2xl border border-red-200 shadow-sm">
                <div class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-wider text-red-600 mb-2">Stage 2: Central Nervous System Invasion (Days 10–30)</div>
                <ul class="space-y-2 text-xs text-slate-700 list-disc list-inside">
                  <li><strong>Myoclonus (Involuntary Muscle Tics):</strong> Rhythmic, continuous twitching of leg muscles or jaw muscles even during sleep.</li>
                  <li><strong>&quot;Chewing-Gum&quot; Seizures:</strong> Rhythmic jaw chomping with frothing at the mouth and vacant staring.</li>
                  <li><strong>Head Tilt &amp; Ataxia:</strong> Loss of balance, circling in one direction, and inability to stand.</li>
                  <li><strong>Paralysis:</strong> Hind-leg weakness progressing to complete tetraparesis.</li>
                  <li><strong>Optic Neuritis:</strong> Sudden blindness or permanently dilated pupils.</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 mb-3">3. Distemper vs. Parvovirus: Detailed Clinical Comparison</h2>
            <p>
              Many pet owners in Pakistan ask us: <em>&quot;Is distemper the same as parvo?&quot;</em> or <em>&quot;What is the difference between distemper parvo?&quot;</em> While both are life-threatening viral diseases that frequently infect unvaccinated puppies between 6 weeks and 6 months of age, their pathophysiology differs fundamentally:
            </p>

            <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-4">
              <table class="w-full text-xs text-left border-collapse bg-white rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm border border-slate-200">
                <thead class="bg-slate-900 text-white uppercase text-[10px] tracking-wider">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="py-3 px-4">Feature</th>
                    <th class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-400">Canine Distemper (CDV)</th>
                    <th class="py-3 px-4 text-amber-400">Canine Parvovirus (CPV-2)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100 text-slate-700">
                  <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold bg-slate-50">Causative Virus</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Paramyxovirus (closely related to human measles)</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Parvoviridae (ultra-hardy non-enveloped DNA virus)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold bg-slate-50">Primary Target Organs</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Respiratory tract, gastrointestinal system, epithelial cells &amp; CNS (Brain/Spinal Cord)</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Rapidly dividing cells: Intestinal crypt villi, bone marrow &amp; cardiac muscle</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold bg-slate-50">Dominant Symptom</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Yellow eye discharge, coughing, pneumonia, followed by jaw seizures &amp; muscle twitches</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Violent projectile vomiting and putrid, foul-smelling hemorrhagic (bloody) diarrhea</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold bg-slate-50">Transmission Route</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Airborne respiratory droplets, aerosolized sneezing &amp; oral contact</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Fecal-oral route (ingestion of microscopic infected feces)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold bg-slate-50">Nervous System Damage</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">High incidence (30%–50% develop permanent chorea/seizures)</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Extremely rare (neurological symptoms only occur from severe hypoglycemia/sepsis)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold bg-slate-50">Survival Rate with Early Treatment</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">50% to 75% (drops to &lt;20% once severe neurological seizures manifest)</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">80% to 92% with aggressive IV saline, antiemetics, and hospital drips</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold bg-slate-50">Prevention</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">DHPPi 5-in-1 / 7-in-1 core vaccine series</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">DHPPi 5-in-1 / 7-in-1 core vaccine series</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 mb-3">4. Clinical Treatment Protocol in Pakistan</h2>
            <p>
              There is no specific antiviral drug that can kill the canine distemper virus directly once inside the host. Medical management relies on <strong>intensive supportive veterinary therapy</strong> to sustain the dog's vital organs while its immune system synthesizes neutralizing antibodies:
            </p>

            <div class="space-y-4 mt-4">
              <div class="p-4 bg-emerald-50 rounded-2xl border border-emerald-200">
                <h3 class="font-bold text-emerald-950 text-sm mb-1">A. Intravenous Fluid Resuscitation &amp; Electrolytes</h3>
                <p class="text-xs text-emerald-900 leading-relaxed">
                  Lactated Ringer's Solution (R/L) or Normal Saline (0.9% NaCl) spiked with 5% Dextrose, Potassium Chloride (KCl), and B-complex vitamins is administered via continuous IV catheter. This combats dehydration from anorexia and fever.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="p-4 bg-emerald-50 rounded-2xl border border-emerald-200">
                <h3 class="font-bold text-emerald-950 text-sm mb-1">B. Broad-Spectrum Antibiotic Coverage</h3>
                <p class="text-xs text-emerald-900 leading-relaxed">
                  CDV destroys the lymphoid tissue and immune white blood cells, leaving the lungs vulnerable to secondary opportunistic bacteria (e.g., <em>Bordetella bronchiseptica</em>). Injectable combinations such as Ceftriaxone, Amoxicillin-Clavulanate, or Enrofloxacin are administered to arrest fatal bronchopneumonia.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="p-4 bg-emerald-50 rounded-2xl border border-emerald-200">
                <h3 class="font-bold text-emerald-950 text-sm mb-1">C. Neurological Protection &amp; Anticonvulsants</h3>
                <p class="text-xs text-emerald-900 leading-relaxed">
                  When myoclonus or seizures start, anti-epileptic medications such as Phenobarbital, Levetiracetam (Keppra), or Potassium Bromide are titrated under strict clinical supervision. High-dose neurotropic vitamins (B1 Thikamine, B6, B12 Neurobion) and Alpha-Lipoic Acid help decelerate demyelination of the spinal cord.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="p-4 bg-emerald-50 rounded-2xl border border-emerald-200">
                <h3 class="font-bold text-emerald-950 text-sm mb-1">D. Respiratory Nebulization Therapy</h3>
                <p class="text-xs text-emerald-900 leading-relaxed">
                  Twice-daily saline nebulization with bronchodilators helps loosen thick mucopurulent crusting inside nasal passages and airways, allowing the puppy to breathe comfortably and regain appetite.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 mb-3">5. Prevention: DHPPi Vaccination Timeline &amp; Cost in Pakistan</h2>
            <p>
              Canine distemper is <strong>100% preventable</strong> through proper vaccination. The DHPPi vaccine protects against 5 core viruses: <strong>D</strong>istemper, <strong>H</strong>epatitis, <strong>P</strong>arvovirus, <strong>P</strong>arainfluenza, and <strong>i</strong>nfectious adenovirus.
            </p>

            <div class="bg-white p-5 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm mt-4">
              <h3 class="font-black text-slate-900 text-sm mb-3">Official Puppy Vaccination Schedule in Pakistan:</h3>
              <ul class="space-y-2 text-xs text-slate-700">
                <li><strong>6 to 8 Weeks Old:</strong> 1st Shot DHPPi (Puppy DP or Vanguard Plus 5/L)</li>
                <li><strong>10 to 12 Weeks Old:</strong> 2nd Shot DHPPi Booster + Leptospirosis (7-in-1)</li>
                <li><strong>14 to 16 Weeks Old:</strong> 3rd Shot DHPPi Booster + Anti-Rabies Vaccine</li>
                <li><strong>Annually (Every 12 Months):</strong> 1x Adult Annual DHPPi Booster + Rabies</li>
              </ul>
              <div class="mt-4 pt-3 border-t border-slate-100 flex items-center justify-between text-xs">
                <span class="text-slate-500 font-semibold">Average Vaccine Cost in Lahore:</span>
                <span class="font-bold text-slate-900">Rs. 3,500 – Rs. 5,500 per shot (including veterinary exam)</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-4">
              For complete booking details or to arrange stress-free home vaccination at your doorstep in Lahore, review our <a href="/services/pet-vaccination-center" class="text-emerald-700 font-bold underline">Pet Vaccination Center Services</a> or our <a href="/services/home-visit-veterinary" class="text-emerald-700 font-bold underline">Mobile Vet House Calls</a>.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 mb-3">6. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
            <div class="space-y-4">
              {faqSchema.mainEntity.map((faq) => (
